  "hints": [
    "The answer is the largest number of meetings in progress at the same moment.",
    "Sort start times and end times separately and sweep through the starts in order.",
    "With a pointer e into the sorted ends: if start >= ends[e], a room frees up (e++); otherwise you need another room."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function roomsNeeded(meetings) {\n  const starts = meetings.map(m => m[0]).sort((a, b) => a - b)\n  const ends = meetings.map(m => m[1]).sort((a, b) => a - b)\n  let rooms = 0\n  let e = 0\n  for (const start of starts) {\n    if (start >= ends[e]) e++\n    else rooms++\n  }\n  return rooms\n}\n",
    "py": "def rooms_needed(meetings):\n    starts = sorted(m[0] for m in meetings)\n    ends = sorted(m[1] for m in meetings)\n    rooms = e = 0\n    for start in starts:\n        if start >= ends[e]:\n            e += 1\n        else:\n            rooms += 1\n    return rooms\n",
    "explanation": "Only the order of start and end events matters, not which meeting they belong to. Sweeping starts in order: if the earliest unused end time is at or before this start, that room is reused; otherwise a new room is opened. Rooms only ever grow, so the final count is the peak overlap."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "notes": "A min-heap of end times gives the same bound and also tells you which room each meeting gets. JavaScript's default sort is lexicographic, so a numeric comparator is required."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Assign a concrete room number to each meeting. Which data structure do you need now?",
    "At SSE level: how would a booking service enforce this across concurrent requests?"
  ]
